\description{
Clades (target clades, sister clades, and excluded subclades) of a \code{missing_taxa_list} can be defined by tips
or by the ID of the node of the backbone phylogeny they correspond to.
This function converts all the node IDs into tips, so that the list has the tips-only format required by
\code{\link[=graft_missing_taxa]{phylograftR::graft_missing_taxa()}} and \code{\link[=graft_missing_clades]{phylograftR::graft_missing_clades()}}.
Tips and node IDs can be mixed, within the list and within the items. The conversions are printed for transparency:
the warnings and errors of the grafting functions then refer to tips that can be traced back to the node IDs provided.

The same conversion is done internally by \code{\link[=build_missing_taxa_list]{phylograftR::build_missing_taxa_list()}}
and \code{\link[=import_missing_taxa_list]{phylograftR::import_missing_taxa_list()}} when a table includes node IDs.
}
\details{
\subsection{Node IDs and tips}{

Node IDs are integers: \code{1} to N, with N being the number of tips (\code{ape::Ntip(phy)}), correspond to terminal nodes, and thus define single-tip clades.
Following IDs are the internal nodes, as shown by \code{ape::nodelabels()}, and correspond to clades with multiple descending species.
A node ID is recognized as being exclusively made of digits: either a number (e.g., \code{34}), or a text with only digits (e.g., \code{"34"}, as read from a table).
\strong{Tip labels must therefore include at least one other character than digits} to be distinguished.
The function stops if a node ID is used while some tip labels of \code{phy} are only made of digits. In this case, you need to rename these tips first.

A clade is defined either by tips, or by one node ID. The two cannot be mixed within the same clade (e.g., \code{c("t8", "34")} is an error),
and a node ID alone defines one clade (several node IDs, e.g., \code{c(34, 35)}, are an error). However, different clades can be defined differently.
For example, \code{Excluded_subclades = list(X1 = list(50, c("t5", "t12"), "t30"))} mixes an internal node ID, a pair of tips and a single tip.

Single tips and clades with mutiple descending species are distinguished by the type of node: a tip node (\verb{<= ape::Ntip(phy)}) is equivalent to the single tip,
i.e., the terminal branch of the tip, and an internal node is equivalent to the clade it subtends.
}

\subsection{Conversion}{

A tip node is converted into the label of the tip. An internal node is converted into one pair of tips whose most recent common ancestor (MRCA) is the node.
The pair is chosen in deterministic fashion: for each of the first two descendant lineages of the node (in the order of \code{phy$edge}), the first tip of each lineage is selected.
This ensures that the MRCA node of this pair always defines the same clade, even when other species are grafted inside.
The conversions are printed, grouped by node ID, with the items and taxa where each node ID was used.
}

\subsection{Same clade, same tips}{

If a clade is already defined by tips in \code{missing_taxa_list} (written by hand, or converted before), a node ID with the same MRCA node is converted
into these tips instead of the default pair, so the same clade is always defined by the same tips. This holds for \code{missing_taxa_list} and \code{total_richness_per_clades}.
\code{total_richness_per_clades} must be converted at the same time as its associated \code{missing_taxa_list} (same call to the function), so that the tips defining a clade match between the two objects.
It can be converted together with the initial \code{missing_taxa_list} (both converted at the same time), or together with the \code{missing_taxa_list} that was already converted,
used as a template (the tips it already uses for the clades are matched to the node IDs).

The function also checks that the structure of the list is valid (items named after \verb{$Taxon_name}), that node IDs are in \code{phy}, and that the tips are in \code{phy}.
It stops with an informative message otherwise.
}
